# About This Project
#
# Litex source:
# Canonical module: scripts/linear_algebra_done_right/textbook
#
# This project is an ongoing Litex formalization inspired by the fourth edition
# of Sheldon Axler's Linear Algebra Done Right. The current draft has
# source-ordered files for Chapters 1 through 9, from vector spaces and linear
# maps through spectral theory, multilinear algebra, determinants, and tensor
# products. It is an independent translation project, not an official edition
# of or substitute for the book.
#
# Translation conventions
#
# Standalone items explicitly labelled Exercise or Exercises are omitted
# without declarations or coverage placeholders. A named non-exercise result
# is retained when the source leaves its proof, or one proof step, as an
# exercise; the omitted argument is then marked locally as proof debt.
#
# This is not a line-by-line transcription. It preserves source-facing
# definitions and results in source order while using Litex builtins and
# established interfaces for familiar scalars, arithmetic, sets, functions,
# and finite objects. Source constructions may be recorded as alternate proof
# routes rather than rebuilt as dependencies. Routine suppressed reasoning may
# be made explicit; a long finite, algebraic, spectral, or analytic argument
# may instead be deferred when its infrastructure would overwhelm the main
# exposition.
#
# Coverage and proof status are separate. An included declaration may be
# checked, may rely on an explicit `axiom`, or may contain a narrowly localized
# `trust`. Axiom and trust boundaries are assumptions or unfinished proof debt,
# not verifier-checked proofs. The existence of all chapter files therefore
# does not claim that every non-exercise source result or every proof is
# complete.
#
# Most declarations included in this draft are accepted by the Litex verifier.
# The remaining unchecked steps are kept visible as axiom or trust boundaries
# rather than being presented as completed proofs. Some arise where textbook
# prose suppresses routine background arguments; others concern finite-list
# recursion, exchange and deletion arguments, or existence results that still
# need better library support or further formalization. We make implicit steps
# explicit when they help reveal the structure, but sometimes retain a marked
# omission to respect the book's pace and keep the development focused.
#
# The constructions of complex scalars, vector spaces, coordinate spaces,
# spans, bases, and dimension show how Litex represents algebraic structures as
# concrete mathematical data with named laws and directly reusable facts.
#
# We hope readers gain both an introduction to the architecture of linear
# algebra and an honest example of how a textbook chapter can be translated
# into readable checked mathematics without concealing its unfinished edges.
